  'A ring of sod walls thrown up in September 1864 by emigrants bound for the Montana gold fields, whose wagon train was attacked by Hunkpapa Lakota warriors. They stacked sod 6.5 feet high in a circle about 300 feet across, named it for Corporal Jefferson Dilts, who died of his wounds, and held out until the cavalry came from Fort Rice on September 20, after a siege of sixteen days.',
  'The state says the site looks much as it did in 1864: the sod wall, wagon ruts, graves and an unfinished well can still be seen, and digging showed the ground is undisturbed. The only modern things are a flagpole, a marker, a registration box, a fence and a sign north of the fort. Nothing is built; it is grassland.',
  'A sod circle where a wagon train sat out a sixteen-day siege.',
